<section class="wrap block"><h2 class="h">Why harvest rainwater</h2>
 <ul class="why"><li><span class="ico">💧</span><div><h3>Lower water bills</h3><p>Stored rain covers gardening, cleaning and flushing, often a third of a home’s water use.</p></div></li><li><span class="ico">🌱</span><div><h3>Refills groundwater</h3><p>A recharge pit sends monsoon runoff underground instead of down the drain.</p></div></li><li><span class="ico">🛟</span><div><h3>Backup in dry spells</h3><p>A full tank keeps taps running when tankers are late or supply is cut.</p></div></li><li><span class="ico">🌊</span><div><h3>Less waterlogging</h3><p>Capturing roof runoff eases pressure on street drains in heavy rain.</p></div></li></ul></section>

<section class="wrap block"><h2 class="h">How a system works</h2>
 <ol class="flow"><li><h3>Catch</h3><p>Keep the roof clean and sloped so rain runs to one edge.</p></li><li><h3>Carry</h3><p>Gutters and down pipes with a mesh guard move water without leaves.</p></li><li><h3>Clean</h3><p>A first-flush diverter drops the dirty first rain, then a sand and charcoal filter polishes the rest.</p></li><li><h3>Keep</h3><p>Send water to a covered tank for use or to a recharge pit for the ground.</p></li></ol></section>

<section class="wrap block"><h2 class="h">Roof type and efficiency</h2>
 <div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Roof type</th><th>Typical efficiency</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Metal sheet</td><td>85–90%</td><td>Smooth, sheds water fast. Best pick for drinking after treatment.</td></tr><tr><td>Concrete (RCC)</td><td>75–85%</td><td>Most common. Keep the terrace swept and slope the drains.</td></tr><tr><td>Clay tiles</td><td>65–75%</td><td>Rough surface holds some water and dust.</td></tr><tr><td>Terrace garden</td><td>30–50%</td><td>Soil soaks up rain. Good for plants, not for storage.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

<section class="wrap block faq"><h2 class="h">Common questions</h2>
 <details><summary>How is the harvest calculated?</summary><p>Roof area in square metres × yearly rainfall in millimetres × efficiency. One millimetre of rain on one square metre of roof gives one litre.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Is rainwater safe to drink?</summary><p>Not straight off the roof. Use a first-flush diverter, a sand and charcoal filter, then disinfect by boiling, chlorine or UV. Keep the tank covered. For drinking, ask a local water-quality lab to test it.</p></details>
 <details><summary>How accurate are these numbers?</summary><p>They are planning estimates. City rainfall is an approximate long-term average, and real rain changes every year. Use your local weather office figure when you have it.</p></details>
 <details><summary>How is tank size decided?</summary><p>Daily household need × dry-period days, but never more than half of the yearly harvest, rounded up to a common tank size.</p></details>
 <div class="band"><div><h2>Ready to catch the next monsoon?</h2><p>Run the numbers, then plan your tank and recharge pit before the rains arrive.</p></div><a class="btn gold" href="#calculator">Back to calculator</a></div>
</section>
